Article 3 Where a person with an execution obligation is capable of executing but refuses to do so, and falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed as "other circumstances where a person who is capable of executing but refuses to do so, and the circumstances are serious" as provided in the Interpretation of the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress on Article 313 of the Criminal Law:
(1) maliciously disposing of property rights and interests without consideration by means such as abandoning claims or security interests for claims, or maliciously extending the performance period of matured claims, or disposing of property rights and interests by means such as false settlement or false transfer, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(2) engaging in acts that maliciously diminish liability property, such as acquiring another person's property at a clearly unreasonable high price or providing security for another person's debts, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(3) forging, destroying, or concealing important evidence concerning the ability to perform, preventing others from testifying by violence, threat, or bribery, or directing, bribing, or coercing others to give false testimony, thereby obstructing the People's Court from ascertaining the property situation of the person with an execution obligation, and rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(4) having acts of refusing to execute, such as refusing to report or falsely reporting property, or violating the People's Court's restriction on consumption order, and still refusing to execute after compulsory measures such as fine or detention have been taken;
(5) after compulsory measures such as fine or detention have been taken, still refusing to deliver the property or negotiable instruments designated by the legal document, or refusing to vacate the house or withdraw from the land, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(6) after compulsory measures such as fine or detention have been taken, still refusing to perform the obligation of act such as assisting in the exercise of personal rights, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable, and the circumstances are flagrant;
(7) after compulsory measures such as fine or detention have been taken, still violating the obligation of omission such as a personal safety protection order or a decision prohibiting engagement in relevant occupations, causing the victim minor injury or higher, or seriously affecting the victim's normal work or life;
(8) obstructing enforcement personnel from entering the enforcement site by means such as intimidation, verbal abuse, gathering a crowd to make a disturbance, or threats, or by passive resistance acts such as pulling or pushing, thereby rendering enforcement work impossible to carry out, and the circumstances are flagrant;
(9) damaging or seizing enforcement case materials, enforcement official vehicles or other enforcement equipment, enforcement personnel uniforms, or enforcement official documents, thereby rendering enforcement work impossible to carry out;
(10) other circumstances where a person is capable of executing but refuses to do so, and the circumstances are serious.

Article 4 Where a person with an execution obligation is capable of executing but refuses to do so, and falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed as "circumstances of especially serious nature":
(1) obstructing enforcement by means such as false litigation, false arbitration, or false notarization, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(2) gathering a crowd to storm the enforcement site, thereby making enforcement work impossible to carry out;
(3) physically attacking enforcement personnel by violent methods such as encirclement, detention, or beating, thereby making enforcement work impossible to carry out;
(4) causing the applicant for enforcement to commit suicide, self-harm, or other serious consequences due to the refusal to execute;
(5) other circumstances of especially serious nature.
